Byte streams in Java provide low‑level, unbuffered access to files as sequences of 8‑bit bytes. `FileInputStream` reads bytes from an existing file; its `read()` method returns the next byte as an `int` (‑1 signals end‑of‑file) or fills a byte array. `FileOutputStream` writes bytes to a file; its `write(int b)` writes the low‑order byte, and `write(byte[] b)` writes the whole array. Both classes implement `Closeable`, so `close()` releases the underlying OS file descriptor. They are concrete subclasses of `InputStream` and `OutputStream` respectively, and they throw `FileNotFoundException` (a checked `IOException`) if the file cannot be opened. Unlike character streams (`FileReader`/`FileWriter`), byte streams do not perform any charset conversion — they treat the file as opaque binary data. This makes them suitable for images, audio, video, serialized objects, and any non‑text format. The typical pattern is:
```
FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("source.dat");
FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream("dest.dat");
int b;
while ((b = fis.read())!= -1) fos.write(b);
fis.close(); fos.close();
```
Buffering (via `BufferedInputStream`/`BufferedOutputStream`) is a performance optimisation covered later; the core concept here is the raw byte‑by‑byte contract.
